  "question": "A question bank contains 15 easy, 10 medium and 5 hard questions. A student is to randomly select 4 questions.\n(a) What is the probability that all 4 are easy?\n(b) What is the probability that exactly 2 are easy and 2 are medium?\n(c) If at least one hard question is selected, what is the probability that it is exactly one hard question?",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "Total questions = 15+10+5 = 30<br />(a) Ways to choose 4 easy: $C(15,4)$<br />Total: $C(30,4)$<br />$P = \\dfrac{C(15,4)}{C(30,4)}$<br /><br />(b) 2 easy: $C(15,2)$. 2 medium: $C(10,2)$. Total: $C(15,2)C(10,2)$<br />So, $P = \\dfrac{C(15,2)C(10,2)}{C(30,4)}$<br /><br />(c) At least one hard chosen. Total ways: $C(30,4) - C(25,4)$<br />Exactly one hard: $C(5,1)C(25,3)$<br />$P = \\dfrac{C(5,1)C(25,3)}{C(30,4)-C(25,4)}$<br /><br />Let's compute: $C(15,4)=1365$, $C(30,4)=27405$<br />$C(15,2)=105$, $C(10,2)=45$, $C(25,3)=2300$, $C(5,1)=5$, $C(25,4)=12650$<br /><br />(a) $P=\\dfrac{1365}{27405}$<br />(b) $P=\\dfrac{105\\times45}{27405}=\\dfrac{4725}{27405}$<br />(c) Numerator: $5\\times2300=11500$; Denominator: $27405-12650=14755$<br />$P=\\dfrac{11500}{14755}$<br /><br />Final answers:<br />(a) $\\dfrac{1365}{27405}$<br />(b) $\\dfrac{4725}{27405}$<br />(c) $\\dfrac{11500}{14755}$",
  "explanation_image": [],
  "options": {
    "A": "(a) $\\frac{1365}{27405}$<br />(b) $\\frac{4725}{27405}$<br />(c) $\\frac{12650}{27405}$",
    "B": "(a) $\\frac{15}{30}$<br />(b) $\\frac{105}{27405}$<br />(c) $\\frac{11500}{27405}$",
